feat 1.0

# SPEC-GRM-001 — the TextMate grammar scopes every word the parser reads as a keyword, in the forms the parser
# reads it, and leaves the same word in prose alone. Adopted by the owner (2026-11-25, recorded in flmnt, workspace
# feature) as drafted: one scenario per form, prose controls, a corpus sweep keyed to §9 of the grammar reference,
# the draft's scope names, and a trailing `#` that is not a comment, following ADR-0040 decision 4 ("Refuse on
# keyword lines").
#
# Before this spec no spec owned packages/feat-grammar. Its tests (tokenize.test.ts, copies.test.ts, language.test.ts)
# pin some scopes and the two byte-identical editor copies (editor/feat.tmLanguage.json,
# editors/vscode/syntaxes/feat.tmLanguage.json). The grammar has fallen behind the language: tokenized with
# vscode-textmate 9.2 and vscode-oniguruma 2.0 (the package's devDependencies) at main 72f71de, these got no
# scope but source.feat (the draft's README, drafts/textmate-grammar/README.md at commit 73d2d4b, has each line
# and its output):
#   `variables:`; an indented `given:` (the opener matches only at column 0, and every corpus given is
#   indented); `random seed 42`; a given `seed eventStore [ … ]` and `seed … from "…"`; `needs SPEC-…`;
#   `register mutation createFlow in …`; `emit to …`; a contract `stream "…"`; `from` in `imports … from`,
#   `seed … from` and `input X from "…"`; a fault's `once`, `times 3`, `until reset`; a dotted record type
#   (`stripe.charge with Charge`) and a lowercase one (`userCreated with User`); a bare `any`; `when` and the
#   command in `when (as admin): DoThing { … }`; `touches src/**` written without indentation (indentation is
#   conventional, grammar reference §1.2).
# And three are scoped wrongly:
#   the spec-variable reference: the `placeholders` rule matches `{$x}` (`\{\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\}`,
#   feat.tmLanguage.json, repository.placeholders), never `${x}` (VAR_REF in packages/feat-core/src/parse.ts:658
#   is /\$\{([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\}/), and `${x}` is only written inside a string, which the `strings` rule
#   `"[^"]*"` scopes whole, so no rule can reach it;
#   a string with an escaped quote, `"a\"b"`: `"[^"]*"` ends the string at the escaped quote;
#   `deliver … to response`: `response` is scoped variable.other.service.feat, a service, which ADR-0036 says
#   it never is.
# A sweep of the corpus (a scratch script: every line whose first word is a §9 keyword, outside a keyword
# used as a value-block field, and one prose line set aside) found 47 such lines with no scope but source.feat.
#
# Driven through packages/feat-grammar/fixtures/probe.mjs (part of the build, not shipped). It loads
# feat.tmLanguage.json with vscode-textmate and vscode-oniguruma, as tokenize.test.ts does.
#   `Tokenize { line, at }` tokenizes `line` alone (a fresh rule stack) and answers `scopes`: the scopes of the
#   token holding the first occurrence of `at`, without source.feat, space-joined ("" for none); `at` not in
#   the line is ERR NOT_IN_LINE. `Tokenize { file, lineNo, column }` tokenizes the file up to that line (the rule
#   stack carried) and answers the scopes of the token at that 1-based column. A spec cannot write `${name}` in a
#   string without it being a spec-variable reference (ADR-0017; UNKNOWN_VARIABLE), so the reference is checked
#   where the corpus writes one.
#   `SweepCorpus {}` tokenizes every corpus exemplar line by line (the rule stack carried, as an editor does)
#   and answers `unscoped`: every line the parser reads by a keyword whose keyword token has no scope but
#   source.feat, as "<exemplar>:<line>:<word>", comma-joined ("" for none), and `checked`, how many lines it
#   looked at. "The parser reads by a keyword" is decided by feat-core's own section rules: a header line, a
#   section opener, a contract or variables line, a scenario line, and a construct line in a typed form; the
#   keywords are the ones the grammar reference lists in §9, read from docs/grammar-reference.md at run time,
#   so a keyword added to the reference is swept without a change to the probe.

spec SPEC-GRM-001 "TextMate grammar: every keyword the parser reads is scoped"
context Toolchain
aggregate Editor
type infrastructure
status agreed

construct:
  handler at packages/feat-grammar/feat.tmLanguage.json
  touches packages/feat-grammar/**
  touches editor/feat.tmLanguage.json
  touches editors/vscode/syntaxes/feat.tmLanguage.json
  touches feat.config.json
  Edit packages/feat-grammar/feat.tmLanguage.json only, then refresh both copies with
  editors/vscode/scripts-sync-grammar.mjs (copies.test.ts holds them byte-identical). Scopes follow the
  grammar's existing names: section openers keyword.control.section.feat; construct, contract and given
  directives, `from`, `in` and `random seed` keyword.other.directive.feat; a fault count (`once`, `times`,
  `until reset`) keyword.other.fault-count.feat; record types entity.name.type.record.feat; `any`
  support.function.matcher.feat; a spec-variable reference variable.parameter.placeholder.feat, inside the
  string that holds it; a string escape constant.character.escape.feat; `to response` keyword.other.response.feat.
  Strings become a begin/end rule with escapes and `${name}` inside it. A line pattern does not require
  leading indentation. A comment is `#` as the first non-blank character only. feat.config.json gains the
  commands Tokenize and SweepCorpus routed to packages/feat-grammar/fixtures/probe.mjs.

enforce:
  scope every keyword the parser reads, in the forms it reads it
  leave a keyword's word unscoped where the parser reads it as prose
  sweep the corpus against the reference's keyword list, never against a list kept in the probe
  scope a comment only where the parser skips one

contract:
  input TokenizeInput
  input SweepInput
  response TokenizeResult
  response SweepResult
  error TokenizeError

scenario outline "a keyword the parser reads is scoped":
  when: Tokenize { line: <line>, at: <at> }
  predict success:
    response OK TokenizeResult { scopes: matching <scope> }
    filesystem has []
  examples:
    | line                                                          | at              | scope                                    |
    | "variables:"                                                  | "variables"     | "keyword\\.control\\.section\\.feat"     |
    | "  given:"                                                    | "given"         | "keyword\\.control\\.section\\.feat"     |
    | "    random seed 42"                                          | "random"        | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "    seed eventStore [ Wrote with Row { a: 1 } ]"             | "seed"          | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "    seed eventStore from \"fixtures/x.json\""                | "from"          | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "  needs SPEC-USR-001"                                        | "needs"         | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "  register mutation createFlow in resolvers/mutations.ts"    | "register"      | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "  emit to automation-flow-{flowId}"                          | "emit to"       | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "  stream \"automation-flow-*\""                              | "stream"        | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "  imports FlowAggregate from core/domain/flow"               | "from"          | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "touches src/**"                                              | "touches"       | "keyword\\.other\\.directive\\.feat"     |
    | "    fault store Append Timeout once"                         | "once"          | "keyword\\.other\\.fault-count\\.feat"   |
    | "    fault store Append Timeout times 3"                      | "times"         | "keyword\\.other\\.fault-count\\.feat"   |
    | "    fault store Append Timeout until reset"                  | "until reset"   | "keyword\\.other\\.fault-count\\.feat"   |
    | "    payments has [ stripe.charge with Charge { a: 1 } ]"     | "stripe.charge" | "entity\\.name\\.type\\.record\\.feat"   |
    | "    eventStore has [ userCreated with User { a: 1 } ]"       | "userCreated"   | "entity\\.name\\.type\\.record\\.feat"   |
    | "    response OK R { id: any }"                               | "any"           | "support\\.function\\.matcher\\.feat"    |
    | "  when (as admin): DoThing { a: 1 }"                         | "when"          | "keyword\\.control\\.trigger\\.feat"     |
    | "  when (as admin): DoThing { a: 1 }"                         | "DoThing"       | "entity\\.name\\.function\\.command\\.feat" |
    | "  when: Join { email: \"a\\\"b\", n: 1 }"                    | "b\""           | "string\\.quoted\\.double\\.feat"        |
    | "  when: Join { email: \"a\\\"b\", n: 1 }"                    | "\\\""          | "constant\\.character\\.escape\\.feat"   |
    | "  deliver OrderShipped { id: 1 } to response"                | "response"      | "keyword\\.other\\.response\\.feat"      |
    | "  # a whole-line comment"                                    | "#"             | "comment\\.line\\.number-sign\\.feat"    |

scenario "a spec-variable reference inside a string is scoped as one":
  when: Tokenize { file: "corpus/waitlist-variables/waitlist-variables.feat", lineNo: 36, column: 32 }
  predict success:
    response OK TokenizeResult { scopes: matching "string\\.quoted\\.double\\.feat variable\\.parameter\\.placeholder\\.feat" }
    filesystem has []

scenario outline "a word the parser reads as prose, or as data, is not scoped as a keyword":
  when: Tokenize { line: <line>, at: <at> }
  predict success:
    response OK TokenizeResult { scopes: not matching <scope> }
    filesystem has []
  examples:
    | line                                                    | at        | scope                                 |
    | "  when: Join { email: \"{$email}\" }"                  | "{$email}" | "placeholder"                         |
    | "  deliver OrderShipped { id: 1 } to response"          | "response" | "variable\\.other\\.service"          |
    | "  seed the cache before the first request"             | "seed"     | "keyword"                             |
    | "  the flow runs once a day"                            | "once"     | "keyword"                             |
    | "  pick any slot that is free"                          | "any"      | "support\\.function\\.matcher"        |
    | "  when: Join { title: \"#1\" }"                        | "#1"       | "comment"                             |
    | "    response OK R { ok: true }   # the answer"         | "#"        | "comment"                             |

scenario "every line of the corpus that the parser reads by a keyword is scoped":
  when: SweepCorpus {}
  predict success:
    response OK SweepResult { unscoped: "", checked: any number }
    filesystem has []
